Career Vision Statement Activity
This activity guides you through a structured writing process to develop a meaningful Career Vision Statement. You will build it in layers — starting with raw material from your previous work, developing a draft, refining it, and then committing to a final version.

Step 1 — Gather Your Raw Material
Before writing, pull together the following from your previous work:

Your Personal Empathy Map insights
Your SWOT Analysis
Your Personal POV Statement
Your RIASEC Profile
Your Career Exploration Map
Step 2 — Answer the Foundation Questions
What kind of work do I want to do? (Think about tasks, not job titles. What activities do you want to fill your professional days with? What problems do you want to work on?) [Your response here]

I want to do work where I am solving problems, analyzing information, and helping make decisions. I enjoy working with other people and want a career where I can use business and financial knowledge while also using technology.
I do not want to do the exact same thing every day, so having different projects and problems to work through is important to me.

Who do I want to serve or impact? (Who are the people, communities, or sectors your work will benefit? Be as specific as you can.) [Your response here]

I want to work with businesses and the people within them to help them solve problems and make better decisions. I would like my work to have an impact that I can actually see.

What values do I want my career to embody? 

I want my career to involve integrity, growth, leadership, collaboration, and continuous learning.

What kind of professional do I want to become? (What qualities, character traits, or reputation do you want to be known for in your field?)

I want to become someone who people trust to solve problems and get things done. I want to be known as dependable, hardworking, and someone who works well with different types of people.

What does success look like for me — not financially, but meaningfully? (Set salary aside for a moment. What would make you feel that your career was truly worthwhile?) 

Success would mean having a career that continues to challenge me and gives me opportunities to grow. I want to be able to look at the work I have done and know that I helped or made something better.

Step 3 — Draft Your Career Vision Statement
Using your foundation questions as raw material, write a first draft of your Career Vision Statement. Aim for 3–5 sentences. Use the structure below as a guide — but feel free to depart from it once you have a draft:

"My career vision is to [type of work] for/with [who you serve], so that [impact or outcome you want to create]. I want to be known as a professional who [qualities/values]. I see myself building a career in [field or sector] where I can [specific contribution], grounded in my commitment to [core value]."

My Draft Career Vision Statement: 

My career vision is to build a career where I can use business and technology to solve problems and help organizations make better decisions.
I want to work on different challenges that push me to continue learning and developing new skills. I want to become someone who is trusted for my judgment, work ethic, and ability to work with others.

Step 4 — Refine and Finalize
Read your draft aloud (or read it slowly in your head). Ask yourself:

Does this sound like me, or does it sound like what I think I should say?
Is it specific enough to be meaningful?
Is it broad enough to allow for growth?
Does it excite me when I read it?
Revise as needed and write your final version below.

My Final Career Vision Statement:

My career vision is to build a career that allows me to solve meaningful business problems using financial knowledge, technology, and critical thinking.
I want to work in an environment where I am constantly learning, taking on new challenges, and working with other people to make better decisions.
I want to be known as someone who is dependable, adaptable, and willing to step up when there is a problem to solve.
Most importantly, I want to build a career where I can see the impact of my work and continue growing as both a professional and a person.

💡 Note: Your Career Vision Statement is a living document. You are not locked into it forever. You will revisit and revise it in Module 5 and beyond as your self-knowledge deepens and your career exploration continues.
